# The native TypeScript program

TypeScript 7 is the TypeScript compiler rewritten in Go. It runs natively and spreads its work across the processor's cores. On the site's own project it builds a program and answers the first hover in about an eighth of a second, where TypeScript 6 takes more than two. That wait is most of the time an editor spends before it can answer anything about a freshly opened chapter.

TypeScript 7 ships no programmatic API for an editor's questions, so the product program cannot call it the way it calls TypeScript 6. It ships a language server instead, `tsc --lsp`. `createNativeProgram` is a `ProductProgram` that asks that server, through the bridge the previous chapter builds.

## Which projects use it

A project uses TypeScript 7 only when it has chosen TypeScript 7 itself, by installing it. TypeScript 7 removes options that TypeScript 6 still accepts, such as `baseUrl`, `target: es5` and `moduleResolution: node`, and it ignores a `tsconfig.json`'s language-service plugins. A project still written for TypeScript 6 would meet errors in its own configuration, and its editor would disagree with its own build. So every other project keeps the TypeScript 6 program, exactly as before.

`nativeCompilerFor` makes the choice for a project. It looks for the `typescript` package the project would import, starting from the folder of the project's `tsconfig.json`, or from its loom's folder when no `tsconfig.json` governs it. A package whose major version is 7 or later is TypeScript 7. Its native executable lives in a companion package named for the platform, such as `@typescript/typescript-darwin-arm64`, in that package's `lib` folder, under the name `tsc`, or `tsc.exe` on Windows. `nativeExecutable` finds it the way TypeScript's own launcher does, from beside the `typescript` package. A project without TypeScript 7, or a platform without its companion package, gets nothing, and so gets TypeScript 6.

A person can also try TypeScript 7 before a project installs it. The `nativeCompiler` setting of the `typescript` language, in `.loom/config.yaml`, names the folder of a TypeScript 7 `typescript` package, and every project then uses that compiler. `nativeCompilerFor` takes the setting first when it is given.

=>

const majorVersionOf = (packageJson: string): number =>
  Number(String(JSON.parse(readFileSync(packageJson, 'utf8')).version ?? '0').split('.')[0])

const nativeExecutable = (typescriptPackageJson: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftThrowable(() =>
      createRequire(typescriptPackageJson).resolve(
        `@typescript/typescript-${process.platform}-${process.arch}/package.json`,
      ),
    )(),
    Option.map((companion) =>
      join(dirname(companion), 'lib', process.platform === 'win32' ? 'tsc.exe' : 'tsc'),
    ),
    Option.filter(existsSync),
  )

const typescriptPackageFrom = (folder: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.liftThrowable(() =>
    createRequire(join(folder, 'package.json')).resolve('typescript/package.json'),
  )()

export const nativeCompilerFor = (
  project: ProductProject,
  setting: Option.Option<string>,
): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.map(setting, (folder) => join(folder, 'package.json')),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      typescriptPackageFrom(
        Option.match(project.config, { onNone: () => dirname(project.key), onSome: dirname }),
      ),
    ),
    Option.filter((packageJson) => majorVersionOf(packageJson) >= 7),
    Option.flatMap(nativeExecutable),
  )

## Asking the native server

`createNativeProgram` holds the roots it is given and starts the server only when the first question needs it. A project on TypeScript 7 is on TypeScript 7 entirely: every question about its roots goes to the native server, and the project never builds a TypeScript 6 program. Where TypeScript 7 offers less than TypeScript 6, such as refactors, the project gets what TypeScript 7 offers. That is the truth about the compiler the project chose, and it grows as TypeScript 7 does.

The native server sees exactly what `tsc` sees. A root its `tsconfig.json` includes belongs to the project. A root the configuration leaves out is a loose file to TypeScript 7, checked with default options, as it would be to the project's own `tsc`. A project whose tests sit outside its `include` fixes that in its configuration, by including them.

TypeScript 7's language server is a workspace server: one process holds every project of the workspace, assigns each open file to the project whose `tsconfig.json` includes it, and parses the declarations the projects share, Effect's among them, once for all of them. So the plugin keeps one native program per compiler, shared by every project on TypeScript 7, rather than one per project. A process per project would parse the shared declarations again in each, start each with a burst on every core, and hold several times the memory.

The program loads the whole workspace into the server once, early, the way a language server for Scala or Rust indexes a project when it opens. Loading takes seconds and every core, and done on demand it would land on a person's first search for references, holding up every hover and go-to-definition behind it. Done once, in the background, it leaves a warm codebase in which every later question answers at once.

`sync` brings the server up to date with the composition the plugin hands it, the roots of the project being asked about and every other root. Before the warm-up it keeps open the roots of the projects asked about. After the warm-up it keeps every root open. A root closes only when it leaves the composition.

`warm` runs once, after the first question is answered, so it never delays that answer. It opens every root of the composition, then asks the server for each root's diagnostics, one root at a time. Asking makes TypeScript 7 load and check the root's project. One request at a time keeps the warm-up from crowding out the person's own questions: a question asked meanwhile waits for one root's check at most, not for the whole workspace. A failed request during the warm-up is ignored, since the person's own questions will surface any real failure.

`running` gives the server, starting it the first time a question needs it, in the folder of the project's `tsconfig.json`, and opening the roots `sync` last recorded. It tells the server what the plugin can take: hover in Markdown, definitions as links, pulled diagnostics, completions, and code actions whose edits are resolved only when picked.

A server that stops is started again on the next question, so a crash costs one cold start rather than the session. After `maxRestarts` restarts the program gives up. Every question then fails with an error that names the compiler and says it stopped, and Volar writes that error to the editor's log of the language server. The project never switches to another compiler behind the person's back.

=>

const maxRestarts = 3

export const createNativeProgram = (compiler: string, workspace: string): ProductProgram => {
  const held: {
    open: ReadonlyMap<string, ProductFile>
    latest: ReadonlyMap<string, ProductFile>
    warmed: boolean
  } = { open: new Map(), latest: new Map(), warmed: false }
  const started: { server: Option.Option<LanguageServerProcess>; restarts: number } = {
    server: Option.none(),
    restarts: 0,
  }

  const start = (): LanguageServerProcess => {
    const server = startLanguageServer(compiler, ['--lsp', '--stdio'], workspace, 'typescript', {
      textDocument: {
        hover: { contentFormat: ['markdown', 'plaintext'] },
        definition: { linkSupport: true },
        diagnostic: {},
        completion: { completionItem: { documentationFormat: ['markdown', 'plaintext'] } },
        codeAction: {
          codeActionLiteralSupport: {
            codeActionKind: { valueSet: ['quickfix', 'refactor', 'source'] },
          },
          resolveSupport: { properties: ['edit'] },
          dataSupport: true,
        },
        rename: {},
        references: {},
      },
    })
    started.server = Option.some(server)
    server.keepOpen(Array.fromIterable(held.open.values()))
    return server
  }

  const restart = (): Option.Option<LanguageServerProcess> => {
    if (started.restarts >= maxRestarts) return Option.none()
    started.restarts += 1
    return Option.some(start())
  }

  const running = (): Option.Option<LanguageServerProcess> =>
    Option.match(started.server, {
      onNone: () => Option.some(start()),
      onSome: (server) =>
        Option.orElse(Option.liftPredicate(server, (live) => live.alive()), restart),
    })

  const openAll = (): void => {
    held.open = held.latest
    Option.map(started.server, (server) => server.keepOpen(Array.fromIterable(held.open.values())))
  }

  const warmOne = (path: string): Promise<void> =>
    Option.match(running(), {
      onNone: () => Promise.resolve(),
      onSome: (server) =>
        server
          .request('textDocument/diagnostic', { textDocument: { uri: URI.file(path).toString() } })
          .then(
            () => undefined,
            () => undefined,
          ),
    })

  const warm = (): void => {
    if (held.warmed) return
    held.warmed = true
    openAll()
    void Array.reduce(Array.fromIterable(held.latest.keys()), Promise.resolve(), (previous, path) =>
      previous.then(() => warmOne(path)),
    )
  }

  const ask = <A>(method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
    Option.match(running(), {
      onNone: () =>
        Promise.reject(
          new Error(
            `TypeScript 7 (${compiler}) stopped ${maxRestarts + 1} times in ${workspace}; ` +
              'its features are off until the Loom language server restarts',
          ),
        ),
      onSome: (server) => {
        const answer = server.request<A>(method, params)
        void answer.then(warm, warm)
        return answer
      },
    })

~

The program forwards each question in the form LSP gives it. A definition may come back as locations rather than links, and `asLinks` turns each location into a link to the same place, the form the plugin rewrites. Diagnostics come back as a report, and the program hands on its items. Completions may come back as a bare list of items, and `asCompletionList` wraps one in a complete list. Code actions may come with bare commands beside them, and the program keeps only the actions, the form the plugin handles. An action whose edit is left for later goes back to the same server to be resolved. An action that already carries its edit, as TypeScript 7's `Organize Imports` does, is complete, and resolving it hands it back unchanged rather than asking the server, which refuses such a request.

=>

  const document = (fileName: string) => ({ uri: URI.file(fileName).toString() })
  const at = (fileName: string, position: Position) => ({ textDocument: document(fileName), position })

  const asLinks = (found: ReadonlyArray<Location | LocationLink> | Location | null): LocationLink[] =>
    Array.map(found === null ? [] : globalThis.Array.isArray(found) ? found : [found], (each) =>
      'targetUri' in each
        ? each
        : { targetUri: each.uri, targetRange: each.range, targetSelectionRange: each.range },
    )

  const asCompletionList = (found: CompletionList | CompletionItem[] | null): CompletionList =>
    found === null
      ? { isIncomplete: false, items: [] }
      : globalThis.Array.isArray(found)
        ? { isIncomplete: false, items: found }
        : found

  return {
    sync: (listed, others = []) => {
      const latest = new Map(Array.map([...listed, ...others], (file) => [file.path, file] as const))
      held.latest = latest
      if (held.warmed) return openAll()
      held.open = new Map([
        ...Array.getSomes(
          Array.map(Array.fromIterable(held.open.keys()), (path) =>
            Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(latest.get(path)), (file) => [path, file] as const),
          ),
        ),
        ...Array.map(listed, (file) => [file.path, file] as const),
      ])
      Option.map(started.server, (server) => server.keepOpen(Array.fromIterable(held.open.values())))
    },
    hover: (fileName, position) =>
      ask<Hover | null>('textDocument/hover', at(fileName, position)).then(
        (hover) => hover ?? undefined,
      ),
    definition: (fileName, position) =>
      ask<ReadonlyArray<Location | LocationLink> | Location | null>(
        'textDocument/definition',
        at(fileName, position),
      ).then(asLinks),
    diagnostics: (fileName) =>
      ask<{ readonly items?: Diagnostic[] }>('textDocument/diagnostic', {
        textDocument: document(fileName),
      }).then((report) => report.items ?? []),
    completions: (fileName, position) =>
      ask<CompletionList | CompletionItem[] | null>(
        'textDocument/completion',
        at(fileName, position),
      ).then(asCompletionList),
    references: (fileName, position) =>
      ask<Location[] | null>('textDocument/references', {
        ...at(fileName, position),
        context: { includeDeclaration: true },
      }).then((found) => found ?? undefined),
    rename: (fileName, position, newName) =>
      ask<WorkspaceEdit | null>('textDocument/rename', { ...at(fileName, position), newName }).then(
        (edit) => edit ?? undefined,
      ),
    codeActions: (fileName, range, context) =>
      ask<ReadonlyArray<CodeAction | Command> | null>('textDocument/codeAction', {
        textDocument: document(fileName),
        range,
        context,
      }).then((found) =>
        Array.filter(found ?? [], (each): each is CodeAction => typeof each.command !== 'string'),
      ),
    resolveCodeAction: (action) =>
      action.edit === undefined
        ? ask<CodeAction>('codeAction/resolve', action)
        : Promise.resolve(action),
    dispose: () => {
      Option.map(started.server, (server) => server.dispose())
    },
  }
}

## What the program draws on

Node's `fs`, `module` and `path` find a project's `typescript` package and the native executable beside it. The bridge from the previous chapter starts and speaks to the server, and the product program supplies the `ProductProgram` contract and the `ProductFile` each root syncs as. The service names a `ProductProject`. Volar's types name the answers: `CodeAction`, `Command`, `CompletionItem`, `CompletionList`, `Diagnostic`, `Hover`, `Location`, `LocationLink`, `Position` and `WorkspaceEdit`. `URI` names a root by its `file:` address, and Effect's `Array`, `Option` and `pipe` carry the steps.

=>

import { Array, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import type {
  CodeAction,
  Command,
  CompletionItem,
  CompletionList,
  Diagnostic,
  Hover,
  Location,
  LocationLink,
  Position,
  WorkspaceEdit,
} from '@volar/language-service'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { type LanguageServerProcess, startLanguageServer } from './LanguageServerProcess'
import type { ProductFile, ProductProgram } from './ProductProgram'
import type { ProductProject } from './TypescriptService'

